        "title": "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "bc6d1f0f-aa85-56b7-ab0e-43f131715102",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{x}{e^{3 x}}$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0.$\n\nThe slope is {{field-1}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=e^{3 x}.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(x)^{\\prime}\\cdot (e^{3 x})-(x)\\cdot (e^{3 x})^{\\prime}}{(e^{3 x})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(1)\\cdot (e^{3 x})-(x)\\cdot (3 e^{3 x})}{(e^{3 x})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(1 - 3 x\\right) e^{3 x}}{e^{6 x}}\\\\ &=\\frac{1 - 3 x}{e^{3 x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(0)&=\\frac{1 - 3 (0)}{e^{3 (0)}}\\\\ &=\\frac{1}{1}\\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at $x=0$ is $1.$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
